  sections={[
    {
      h2: 'What coherent breathing is',
      paras: [
        "Coherent breathing is slow, even breathing with no holds: the inhale and the exhale are the same length, and the pace is somewhere around five to six breaths a minute. At 5.5 seconds each way that's 5.5 breaths per minute, and that number isn't arbitrary. Lehrer and Gevirtz, reviewing the biofeedback literature in 2014, put the average frequency for the largest heart rate oscillations at about 5.5 breaths a minute.",
        "You'll see the same technique called resonance breathing, resonant frequency breathing, or just paced breathing at six a minute. Strictly, resonance frequency is personal: the paper notes it varies from individual to individual, around 0.1 Hz. Coherent breathing is the practical version, one steady pace that's close enough for most people, with room to move it.",
        "It's the technique with nothing to hold and nothing to count except the pace, which is why it's the one I'd hand to someone who wants a metronome and nothing else. Box breathing has two holds. 4-7-8 has one long hold and an exhale twice its inhale. Coherent has neither. It's a metronome.",
      ],
    },
    {
      h2: 'How Refresher runs it',
